## Watching and acting on a running stack
```
beam local ps --json                          # every step: state, port, time, detail
beam local ps --restart "scala: auth"       # one step, by name
beam local ps --restart scala                 # a tag: every Scala service
beam local ps --restart --all                 # every service; containers stay up
beam local stop "portal frontend"           # stop one step...
beam local ps --start "portal frontend"     # ...and start it again
beam local logs "c# gateway" -n 100         # a step's log
```
States: Waiting, Building, Up to date, Starting, Restarting, Ready, Running, Degraded, Done, Failed, Stopped. A failed
step doesn't stop the stack; steps that need it report "can't start: X failed". A restart rebuilds first when the
step's sources changed.

Steps are named by kind: `docker: …`, `build: …`, `c# …`, `scala: …`, `portal frontend`, `microservice: <Name>`,
`portal extension: <Name>`, `web: …`, `logs: forward`. Tags select groups of them: `docker`, `csharp`, `scala`,
`scala-core` (what login needs), `scala-csharp` (the Scala the C# hosts call), `portal`, `microservices`,
`extensions`, `web-registry`, `observability`, `custom`.
