## Output without a terminal
Piped or run by an agent, the commands print one JSON line per event instead of the board:
`{"ts":…,"type":"<type>","data":{…}}`. Bare `run` sends `stacks`, `--dry-run` sends `dryRun`, a run sends a line per
step change (`label`, `state`, `elapsedSeconds`), `ps --json` prints only the JSON. Add `--pretty` for readable
timestamped lines instead.

## Logs in Grafana
Stacks with the `observability` tag (`full`, `backend`, `csharp-portal`) run `logs: forward`, which sends every step's
lines to the local Loki with labels `beam_workspace`, `beam_stack`, `beam_step`, `beam_kind` and the line's level.
On the local backend, microservices and extensions also send their own logs, metrics and failed-request traces.
Grafana: `http://localhost:3001` → Dashboards → "Local stack" (one dashboard per kind). `L` on the board opens it.

## Changing what runs
Create or change a stack instead of editing JSON; both check the result against this machine first.
```
beam local create my-stack --extends csharp-portal --exclude scala-csharp --description "C# without extra Scala" --quiet
beam local create csharp-portal:with-stats --after "scala: stats" --description "Plus stats" --quiet
beam local create mine --extends backend --services WalletService --quiet
beam local edit my-stack --exclude "" --quiet
beam local delete my-stack --quiet
```
`--shared` writes the team's folder (a description is required). `edit` takes the same flags as `create`; an empty
value clears the field. Picked services bring what they need (the docker deps, the gateway, the Scala login set, the
microservices an extension calls). Builds are never picked: a stack gets the builds of the steps it runs.

A process of your own (a seed script, a mock server) is a custom step, added to this machine's catalogue with
`beam local setup --add-step "<name>" --step-command … --step-dir "${PortalPath}"` (or `--step-shell`, `--step-beam`)
and then run by name or by the `custom` tag. `beam local setup --list-steps` lists the catalogue.
